WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.048
One bad security content update can crash millions of computers at once. This video is about shipping detection updates safely.

00:00:11.248 --> 00:00:12.177
Pause and think.

00:00:12.577 --> 00:00:15.909
What is the safest way to roll out a new content file?

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:23.623
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:32.323
This video covers how an endpoint security sensor tracks processes, and how to ship detection updates without crashing machines.

00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:36.124
Picture the security desk of a large building.

00:00:36.524 --> 00:00:40.645
Every visitor badge records who let them in. That is the parent process.

00:00:41.045 --> 00:00:45.968
The guard looks for suspicious chains, like a word processor launching a command shell.

00:00:46.368 --> 00:00:52.997
The visitor log has a fixed size. When it's full, the guard counts what was dropped instead of pretending nothing happened.

00:00:53.397 --> 00:00:58.993
And when head office sends new rule cards, they are tried at one desk before they go to every desk.

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.830
Five design rules.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.387
Key each process by its pid and start time, because pids get reused.

00:01:06.787 --> 00:01:11.303
Bound every walk up the ancestry chain, because data can be missing or looped.

00:01:11.703 --> 00:01:16.335
Pass events from kernel to user space through a ring buffer, and count drops.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:24.829
New word: ring buffer. A fixed-size circular queue where the writer wraps around to the start. Picture it like this: A rotating sushi belt.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:38.823
Validate new content against the same parser the sensor runs. CrowdStrike's public root cause analysis of the July twenty twenty four outage describes a template expecting twenty one fields while the sensor supplied twenty.

00:01:39.223 --> 00:01:44.262
Roll out in rings, and fall back to the last known good content after repeated crashes.

00:01:45.462 --> 00:01:45.984
Recap.

00:01:46.384 --> 00:01:49.136
Process identity is pid plus start time.

00:01:49.536 --> 00:01:51.312
Keep walks and buffers bounded.

00:01:51.712 --> 00:01:54.104
The validator must match the real parser.

00:01:54.504 --> 00:01:56.431
Ship in rings with a local fallback.

00:01:56.831 --> 00:02:00.755
Practice in lesson thirty eight point three and design sd twenty eight.

00:02:01.958 --> 00:02:03.212
Back to the question.

00:02:03.612 --> 00:02:06.944
What is the safest way to roll out a new content file?

00:02:07.344 --> 00:02:11.721
Validate it, then ship to a small ring first and widen only if healthy.

00:02:12.921 --> 00:02:13.792
Quick recap.

00:02:14.192 --> 00:02:16.200
Track process parent and child.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.655
Validate content before loading.

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:20.425
Roll out in rings.
